            <ArticleTitle>Relationship between Type A personality and coronary heart disease. Analysis of five cohort studies.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>803-5</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Five prospective cohort studies were compared in order to evaluate the relationship between type A personality (aggressive behavior) and coronary heart disease (CHD). The Western Collaborative Group Study (WCGS) demonstrated increased risk for CHD in males with type A personality. A follow-up study revealed a greater incidence of second myocardial infarction. The Framingham study showed higher rates of CHD in type A men and women. Two studies, MRFIT and a third evaluation of the WCGS, revealed no relationship between type A personality and CHD and mortality due to CHD. Because the studies disagree in the relationship between type A personality and CHD, it is impossible to conclude that type A personality is a risk for CHD.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
